Fetch the game data on first start, six connections at a time.

The app bundle carries no game data. An .ipa with the game in it would be 3.5 GB
to sideload and pointless besides: the bundle is signed and read only, so the
engine could not write a single file into it anyway. Patch 0017 already looks
for downloaded data first and only falls back to the bundle; this is the part
that downloads.

3.5 GB, not the 1.4 GB the upstream release weighs: that number is the tar.xz,
and xz compresses the game's zips by two and a half to one. Undoing that on the
phone would need an xz decoder and a tar reader, neither of which is there, so
what travels is the zips themselves.

Three things about iOS decide the shape of it.

* **There is no aria2c.** No child processes means no aria2c, no curl binary and
  no unzip -- so the transfer happens inside the app, and the data is published
  as plain zips the VFS reads in place (tools/make-data-release.sh). Parallel is
  done the way aria2c does it: the file is cut into 8 MB chunks, six range
  requests are in flight at a time, and each one is written straight into the
  right offset of a file that was truncated to its final size. NSURLSession does
  the transfers -- it is already linked, which libcurl's multi interface would
  also have been, but Foundation brings redirect handling and connection reuse
  along. Its HTTPMaximumConnectionsPerHost has to be raised: the default of four
  would leave two of the six requests waiting.

* **A file may come from several places.** A GitHub release asset may not exceed
  2 GiB and public.zip is 3.5 GB, so it is published in parts of 1792 MiB. They
  are not an archive format and nothing is joined afterwards: each part is
  fetched into its own offset of the same file, exactly as the chunks are. The
  manifest gives every part an offset and a length, and it is checked on the way
  in -- parts must be contiguous, cover the file, and begin on a chunk boundary,
  or a chunk would have to come from two parts at once. 1792 MiB is 224 chunks,
  which is why that is the number.

* **The main thread must go back to the run loop.** A download of 1.2 GB takes
  minutes, and an app that does not answer for that long is killed. So the hook
  in RunGameOrAtlas *returns* -- as the frame does since patch 0023 -- and the
  transfer runs on a utility queue. The progress screen is a UIWindow of its
  own, made here and gone before the engine asks SDL for a window of its own.

* **It will be interrupted.** 3.5 GB is minutes on a good connection and a
  morning on a bad one. A chunk that fails is not a failed download, it is one
  more pass over whatever is still missing; and a launch that does not finish
  leaves a state file behind, one character per chunk, so the next start
  continues instead of starting over. The directory is called "data.part" until
  every hash matches and only then renamed to "data", because Paths::RootData()
  asks nothing more than whether the directory exists.

Two things are checked rather than assumed. The server is asked for 1 KB before
anything big is fetched: one that ignores Range answers with the whole part, and
a data task handed 1.8 GB buffers all of it in memory before the completion
handler ever sees the status code -- so that case falls back to streaming each
part to a file instead. And every file is verified against the SHA-256 from the
manifest, which travels in the bundle: the hashes are the ones this build was
made against, not whatever a server serves today. They are also the hashes of
the upstream files, because nothing here repacks anything.

The manifest is also what keeps the smoke test unchanged. A build with data in
the bundle has data in the bundle, ios_IsDataPresent() says so, and not a byte is
downloaded.

